Lightningpanda patch 0026: fork() with the VMTraps queue thread held, and a child whose watchdog
arms its own timer.

Patch 0002 stops every JavaScriptCore helper thread before a fork except the one that fires
watchdog timers, the VMTraps queue's. It kept running, and a lock it held at the instant of the
fork stayed locked in the child, where that thread does not exist: a watchdog's lock (the child's
timers never notify its VM), a VM's trap lock (the child hangs handling its next trap), a
ParkingLot bucket (whoever parks there next hangs) or an allocator lock (os_unfair_lock aborts
the child). JSPrepareForFork now parks that thread between two of its functions, where it holds
none, until JSDidFork (VMTraps::holdQueueForFork, releaseQueueAfterFork).

Watchdog::startTimer arms no timer while one that fires early enough is pending. A child
inherited that state for a timer on the parent's queue, which never fires there: its first VM
entry within the parent's remaining time ran without a watchdog. A watchdog now remembers the
queue its pending timer is on (m_timerQueue); the child's queue is a new one.

